WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.299
Day 22 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.849 --> 00:00:10.591
A model for security testing (1): threat modeling with the OWASP LLM and Agentic Top

00:00:10.591 --> 00:00:10.796
10.

00:00:11.346 --> 00:00:15.779
How to build the threat model of an AI feature and turn it into testable abuse

00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:16.120
cases.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:22.052
Security testing of an application with models tends to start at the end: someone

00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:26.398
runs an attack tool, gets a long report and doesn't know what to do with it.

00:00:26.698 --> 00:00:31.642
Without a threat model, there's no way to decide which of those findings matter for

00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:35.872
your system, nor which risks went untested because no tool covers them.

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:40.205
Threat modeling is the thinking part before attacking.

00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:44.965
You draw the system, mark where text you don't control comes in and walk through

00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:47.195
lists of known risks to see which apply.

00:00:47.495 --> 00:00:52.523
OWASP maintains two that serve as a guide: one for LLM applications and one for

00:00:52.523 --> 00:00:53.860
agentic applications.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.497
This guide takes you from the drawing to a threat table with concrete abuse cases

00:00:58.497 --> 00:00:59.782
and a decision for each.

00:01:01.082 --> 00:01:02.779
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:07.674
An AI feature of your own or a practice one (a support assistant works).

00:01:08.224 --> 00:01:14.514
The current OWASP GenAI Security Project lists (genai.owasp.org): Top 10 for LLM

00:01:14.514 --> 00:01:19.546
Applications, 2026 edition, and Top 10 for Agentic Applications.

00:01:20.097 --> 00:01:23.257
Only systems you own or are authorized to test.

00:01:24.557 --> 00:01:26.150
Sample threat table.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.756
Chat: the user message is untrusted input.

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.079
Risk: Prompt Injection.

00:01:32.379 --> 00:01:37.583
Abuse case: the user asks to ignore the rules and see another customer's data.

00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:39.407
Covered by a test.

00:01:39.957 --> 00:01:43.361
RAG: an uploaded document is untrusted input.

00:01:43.661 --> 00:01:46.462
Risk: indirect Prompt Injection.

00:01:46.762 --> 00:01:50.445
Abuse case: a document carries hidden instructions.

00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:53.058
Covered by a test plus a control.

00:01:53.608 --> 00:01:56.757
Email tool: model output triggers an action.

00:01:57.057 --> 00:01:59.323
Risk: Excessive Agency.

00:01:59.623 --> 00:02:03.608
Abuse case: the model sends an email without approval.

00:02:03.908 --> 00:02:05.884
Covered by a design control.

00:02:07.184 --> 00:02:08.220
Step 1.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.742
Draw the system.

00:02:10.292 --> 00:02:16.051
Draw the system: inputs (user, documents, web, tools), model, outputs and the

00:02:16.051 --> 00:02:17.771
actions it can execute.

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:23.477
Start with a simple diagram with four kinds of elements: inputs, the model, outputs

00:02:23.477 --> 00:02:24.222
and actions.

00:02:24.522 --> 00:02:29.381
Under inputs, list everything reaching the model: user messages, retrieved

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:33.255
documents, web pages, tool results and system instructions.

00:02:33.555 --> 00:02:38.887
Under actions, everything the system can do besides answering: query a database,

00:02:38.887 --> 00:02:40.953
send an email, modify a record.

00:02:41.253 --> 00:02:43.218
No special tool is needed.

00:02:43.518 --> 00:02:48.630
What matters is that the drawing is made or validated by someone who knows the real

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.430
architecture.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.929
Step 2.

00:02:52.229 --> 00:02:53.764
Trust boundaries.

00:02:54.314 --> 00:02:59.081
Mark trust boundaries: every place where the model reads text your team doesn't

00:02:59.081 --> 00:02:59.564
control.

00:03:00.114 --> 00:03:04.784
On the diagram, mark every place where the model reads text your team neither wrote

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:05.515
nor controls.

00:03:05.815 --> 00:03:08.303
The user message is the obvious case.

00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:13.870
The less obvious ones bring the most trouble: a document uploaded by a customer, the

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:18.950
content of a web page, a free-text field in a ticket, a third-party API response.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:23.833
To a model, all of that is text in its context, and it doesn't reliably tell data

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:24.964
from an instruction.

00:03:25.264 --> 00:03:27.845
Every boundary is a possible entry point.

00:03:29.145 --> 00:03:30.227
Step 3.

00:03:30.527 --> 00:03:33.014
The Top 10 for LLM applications.

00:03:33.564 --> 00:03:38.037
Walk the Top 10 for LLM Applications and flag which risks apply.

00:03:38.337 --> 00:03:43.484
Prompt Injection stays first in the 2026 edition and Sensitive Information

00:03:43.484 --> 00:03:44.736
Disclosure second.

00:03:45.286 --> 00:03:50.394
Walk the list risk by risk and, for each one, ask whether it applies to your

00:03:50.394 --> 00:03:50.931
diagram.

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:56.116
Prompt injection and sensitive information disclosure head the list, and apply to

00:03:56.116 --> 00:03:57.202
almost any system.

00:03:57.502 --> 00:04:02.227
Others depend on the architecture: supply chain risks apply if you use third-party

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:07.010
models or components; insecure output handling, if what the model generates ends up

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.718
being executed or displayed without validation.

00:04:10.018 --> 00:04:13.817
Mark applies or doesn't apply, with one line of justification.

00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:16.130
Step 4.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.010
The Top 10 for agentic applications.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.568
If the system has tools, memory or acts on its own, add the Top 10 for Agentic

00:04:24.568 --> 00:04:29.640
Applications: there the risk is no longer what the model says but what it does.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:35.191
If your system has tools, memory or acts without approval at each step, add the

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:35.951
second list.

00:04:36.251 --> 00:04:40.705
The underlying shift is that the risk stops being what the model says and becomes

00:04:40.705 --> 00:04:41.420
what it does.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.995
Wrong text can be corrected; a sent email or a deleted record can't.

00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:50.590
This is where risks such as tool misuse, excessive permissions and memory

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.766
manipulation appear.

00:04:52.066 --> 00:04:54.902
Check each tool in your diagram against the list.

00:04:56.202 --> 00:04:57.330
Step 5.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.340
Concrete abuse cases.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:04.677
For each applicable risk, write a concrete abuse case: who, through which entry

00:05:04.677 --> 00:05:06.011
point, achieving what.

00:05:06.561 --> 00:05:11.441
For each applicable risk, write an abuse case with three parts: who, through which

00:05:11.441 --> 00:05:13.286
entry point and achieving what.

00:05:13.586 --> 00:05:15.608
"Prompt injection" is a category.

00:05:15.908 --> 00:05:20.428
"A customer uploads a PDF with hidden text instructing the assistant to include

00:05:20.428 --> 00:05:23.632
another customer's data in its answer" is an abuse case.

00:05:23.932 --> 00:05:27.464
The difference is that the second can be turned into a test.

00:05:27.764 --> 00:05:32.317
If you can't write the concrete case, you haven't yet understood how that risk shows

00:05:32.317 --> 00:05:33.293
up in your system.

00:05:34.593 --> 00:05:35.780
Step 6.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.963
Prioritize and decide.

00:05:38.513 --> 00:05:44.074
Prioritize by impact and exposure, and decide what gets automated tests (day 23) and

00:05:44.074 --> 00:05:48.245
what needs a design control: least privilege or human approval.

00:05:48.795 --> 00:05:53.680
Order the cases by impact and exposure: how serious it would be and how easy it is

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.229
to reach that entry point.

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:57.564
Then make a decision for each.

00:05:57.864 --> 00:06:01.605
Some get covered by automated tests, which is tomorrow's topic.

00:06:01.905 --> 00:06:06.677
Others aren't solved by testing but by design: removing a tool from the agent,

00:06:06.677 --> 00:06:11.143
requiring human approval before an irreversible action, limiting possible

00:06:11.143 --> 00:06:11.938
destinations.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:16.792
A design control is worth more than a hundred tests of a risk that shouldn't exist.

00:06:18.092 --> 00:06:19.198
An example.

00:06:19.748 --> 00:06:25.055
A team models a support assistant that reads tickets, queries an orders database and

00:06:25.055 --> 00:06:26.066
can send emails.

00:06:26.366 --> 00:06:31.128
The diagram shows three trust boundaries: the user message, ticket attachments and

00:06:31.128 --> 00:06:32.812
the shipping API's responses.

00:06:33.112 --> 00:06:36.865
Six risks from the first list apply; four from the second.

00:06:37.165 --> 00:06:39.153
They write nine abuse cases.

00:06:39.453 --> 00:06:44.018
The highest-impact one: an attachment with hidden instructions makes the assistant

00:06:44.018 --> 00:06:46.189
email the data of someone else's order.

00:06:46.489 --> 00:06:48.141
They decide two things.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:52.437
The email tool can only write to the ticket's sender, a design control.

00:06:52.737 --> 00:06:55.747
And the other eight cases become automated tests.

00:06:57.047 --> 00:06:58.710
The most common mistakes.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.921
Running an attack tool before having the threat model.

00:07:02.471 --> 00:07:05.724
Considering only the user message as untrusted input.

00:07:06.274 --> 00:07:10.294
Leaving risks as categories, with no concrete abuse cases.

00:07:10.844 --> 00:07:14.771
Trying to cover with tests what is solved by removing a permission.

00:07:16.071 --> 00:07:18.314
To know it is done, check this.

00:07:18.864 --> 00:07:22.594
The diagram was validated by someone who knows the architecture.

00:07:23.144 --> 00:07:25.132
All trust boundaries are marked.

00:07:25.682 --> 00:07:30.282
Each applicable risk has an abuse case with who, where and what it achieves.

00:07:30.832 --> 00:07:35.258
Each case has a decision: test, design control or accepted risk.

00:07:36.558 --> 00:07:38.453
And where AI fell short.

00:07:39.003 --> 00:07:42.907
A threat model generated without your architecture is a generic list.

00:07:43.207 --> 00:07:45.833
The system drawing comes from whoever knows it.

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:48.761
That was day 22.

00:07:49.061 --> 00:07:54.035
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:54.035 --> 00:07:55.043
asked questions.

00:07:55.593 --> 00:08:00.843
Continue with day 23: A model for security testing (2): automated red teaming.
